.TH "TOMO" "1" "August 16, 2026" ""
.SH NAME
tomo \- The programming language of tomorrow.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]tomo\f[R] [\f[I]global flags\f[R]] [\f[I]command\f[R]]
[\f[I]command flags\f[R]] [\f[I]args\&...\f[R]]
.TP
Run a program:
\f[B]tomo\f[R] [\f[B]run\f[R]] \f[I]program.tm\f[R] [\f[B]\-\-\f[R]
\f[I]args\&...\f[R]]
.TP
Build an executable:
\f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[B]build\f[R] [\f[B]\-o\f[R] \f[I]output\f[R]]
\f[I]program.tm\f[R]
.TP
Build a package:
\f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[B]package\f[R] [\f[B]\-o\f[R] \f[I]libname.a\f[R]]
[\f[I]dir\-or\-file\&...\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Tomo is a programming language that is statically typed, compiled,
small, and garbage\-collected, with concise syntax and built\-in support
for high\-performance, low\-overhead datastructures.
It compiles by first outputting C code, which is then compiled using the
Zig toolchain bundled with the Tomo installation.
.PP
A Tomo installation is relocatable: the \f[B]tomo\f[R] binary lives at
\f[I]prefix\f[R]\f[B]/bin/tomo\[at]\f[R]\f[I]version\f[R], and it
locates its libraries, headers, bundled toolchain, and man pages at
startup relative to its own path (the \f[I]prefix\f[R] is the
grandparent directory of the binary), so the whole tree can be moved or
extracted into any prefix without rebuilding.
The prefix is not fixed at build time; set \f[B]$TOMO_PATH\f[R] to
override the automatically determined location (see
\f[B]ENVIRONMENT\f[R]).
.PP
With no command, \f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[I]file.tm\f[R] runs the file (like
\f[B]tomo run\f[R]), and bare \f[B]tomo\f[R] opens a scratch file in
\f[B]$EDITOR\f[R] to edit and run (or compiles and runs a program piped
in on standard input).
.SH COMMANDS
Every command takes a \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]/\f[B]\-v\f[R] flag that
turns on all build logs (the compile steps, up\-to\-date
\[lq]Unchanged\[rq] notices, and the toolchain commands being run).
Commands that print build progress by default (\f[B]build\f[R],
\f[B]package\f[R], \f[B]install\f[R]) also take
\f[B]\-\-quiet\f[R]/\f[B]\-q\f[R] to silence it.
The run\-style commands (\f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[I]file.tm\f[R],
\f[B]run\f[R], \f[B]eval\f[R]) are silent by default: use \f[B]tomo run
\-v\f[R] \f[I]file.tm\f[R] to see the compiler\[cq]s work.
.TP
\f[B]run\f[R] \f[I]file.tm\f[R] [\f[B]\-\-\f[R] \f[I]args\&...\f[R]]
Compile and run the given program.
Anything after \f[B]\-\-\f[R] is passed to the program as its own
arguments.
The command name is optional: \f[B]tomo\f[R] \f[I]file.tm\f[R] does the
same thing.
.TP
\f[B]eval\f[R] \f[I]`expr'\f[R]
Evaluate a Tomo expression and print its result.
The argument may be several statements separated by newlines or
\f[B];\f[R] (for example \f[B]tomo eval \[aq]use random; random.int(1,
100)\[aq]\f[R]); the value of the final statement is printed, with
syntax coloring when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
\f[B]build\f[R] [\f[B]\-o\f[R] \f[I]output\f[R]] [\f[B]\-\-install\f[R]] [\f[B]\-\-prefix\f[R] \f[I]dir\f[R]] [\f[B]\-y\f[R]] \f[I]file.tm\f[R]
Compile the given program to a standalone executable, placed as a
sibling of the \f[B].tm\f[R] file (or at \f[B]\-o\f[R]
\f[I]output\f[R]).
With \f[B]\-\-install\f[R], the executable and its generated manpage are
also copied into a prefix\[cq]s \f[B]bin/\f[R] and \f[B]man/man1/\f[R]
\[em] the installation prefix by default, or \f[B]\-\-prefix\f[R]
\f[I]dir\f[R] to choose another.
Existing files at those destinations are overwritten only after
confirmation, or immediately with \f[B]\-\-yes\f[R]/\f[B]\-y\f[R]; a
warning is printed if the target \f[B]bin/\f[R] is not on your
\f[B]$PATH\f[R].
Remove installed programs again with \f[B]tomo uninstall\f[R]
\f[I]name\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]transpile\f[R] [\f[B]\-\-raw\f[R]] \f[I]file.tm\f[R]
Transpile the given file to C and print the generated header and source
to standard output, each preceded by a \f[B]// file:\f[R] line.
The output is formatted with \f[B]clang\-format\f[R] and (when standard
output is a terminal) syntax\-highlighted with \f[B]bat\f[R], whichever
of the two is installed; \f[B]\-\-raw\f[R] prints the raw generated code
instead.
The generated \f[B].h\f[R]/\f[B].c\f[R] files are also written into the
file\[cq]s \f[B].tomo/\f[R] directory.
.TP
\f[B]parse\f[R] \f[I]file.tm\&...\f[R]
Print the parse tree of the given files as S\-expressions.
.TP
\f[B]fmt\f[R] [\f[B]\-i\f[R]] \f[I]file.tm\&...\f[R]
Autoformat the given files and print them to standard output, or rewrite
them in place with \f[B]\-\-in\-place\f[R]/\f[B]\-i\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]package\f[R] [\f[B]\-o\f[R] \f[I]libname.a\f[R]] [\f[I]dir\-or\-file\&...\f[R]]
Build packages into static archives.
With no arguments, the current directory is built as a package: every
\f[B].tm\f[R] file not starting with an underscore (or dot or digit) is
compiled and archived into \f[B]package.a\f[R].
Directory arguments are each built the same way; \f[B].tm\f[R] file
arguments are compiled into a single archive.
\f[B]\-o\f[R] sets the archive path (single package only).
.TP
\f[B]uninstall\f[R] [\f[B]\-y\f[R]] [\f[I]name\-or\-path\&...\f[R]]
With one or more arguments, uninstall those installed programs.
Each argument is either a bare \f[I]name\f[R] (looked up in the
prefix\[cq]s \f[B]bin/\f[R]) or a path to a binary.
A binary is removed only if it is a Tomo program (it carries Tomo\[cq]s
embedded build info), and its Tomo\-generated manpage in
\f[B]man/man1/\f[R] is removed along with it.
Files Tomo did not create are left untouched, and a missing binary or
manpage is only a warning.
Install programs with \f[B]tomo build \-\-install\f[R].
With no arguments, uninstall this whole Tomo installation: remove this
version\[cq]s files from the installation prefix, along with its
per\-user state, cross\-compilation target packs, and any bundled
toolchains no remaining installation shares.
If other Tomo versions remain in the same prefix, the \f[B]tomo\f[R] and
man page symlinks are repointed to the newest one; otherwise they are
removed too.
If no \f[B]tomo\f[R] remains anywhere on \f[B]$PATH\f[R] afterwards, the
cache (\f[B]\[ti]/.cache/tomo\f[R]: package downloads and the bundled
Zig toolchain\[cq]s compile cache) is also cleared.
Asks for confirmation unless \f[B]\-\-yes\f[R]/\f[B]\-y\f[R] is given.
.TP
\f[B]vendor\f[R] [\f[B]\-e\f[R]|\f[B]\-u\f[R]] \f[I]package\&...\f[R]
Copy the named packages\[cq] digest\-verified source archives into the
current project\[cq]s \f[B]vendor/\f[R] directory and update
\f[B]./packages.ini\f[R] to use the vendored copies as the primary
sources (keeping the digest pins and demoting the previous sources to
fallbacks).
With \f[B]\-\-editable\f[R]/\f[B]\-e\f[R], extract each package\[cq]s
sources into \f[B]vendor/<name>/\f[R] and drop its digest pin, so the
vendored copy can be edited freely.
With \f[B]\-\-unvendor\f[R]/\f[B]\-u\f[R], undo vendoring: restore each
package\[cq]s \f[B]./packages.ini\f[R] entry to its first non\-vendored
fallback source (or the compiler\[cq]s default pin), re\-install the
package into the project\[cq]s package store (re\-pinning the digest if
editable vendoring dropped it), and delete the vendored copy.
.TP
\f[B]version\f[R]
Print the compiler version and exit (with
\f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]/\f[B]\-v\f[R]: plus the git revision).
.TP
\f[B]info\f[R] [\f[B]\-x\f[R]] \f[I]binary\&...\f[R]
Print the build info embedded in the given compiled files (executables
or package archives).
With \f[B]\-\-extract\-source\f[R]/\f[B]\-x\f[R], extract the source
files embedded in each compiled program into a
\f[B]<program>\-source/\f[R] directory instead.
The extracted directory is a working project: it includes the
program\[cq]s sources, \f[B]packages.ini\f[R], license texts, and a
pre\-seeded package store, so it can be rebuilt as\-is without network
access.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options are valid anywhere on the command line, before or after
the command name.
Command\-specific flags (like \f[B]build\f[R]\[cq]s \f[B]\-o\f[R]) are
only valid after their command.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-help\f[R], \f[B]\-h\f[R]
Print the usage (or a command\[cq]s usage) and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-optimization\f[R] \f[B]level\f[R], \f[B]\-O\f[R] \f[B]level\f[R]
Set the optimization level.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-force\-rebuild\f[R], \f[B]\-f\f[R]
Force rebuilding/recompiling.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-source\-mapping=\f[R], \f[B]\-m=\f[R] \f[B]<yes|no>\f[R]
Toggle whether source mapping should be enabled or disabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-target\f[R] \f[I]platform\f[R]
Cross\-compile for another platform instead of this machine\[cq]s.
The target platform\[cq]s libraries are installed on demand from its
Tomo distribution archive.
Cross\-compiled executables get the platform as a filename suffix
(e.g.\ \f[B]foo.aarch64\-macos\f[R]) and cannot be run or installed on
this machine.
Valid platforms are: \f[B]x86_64\-linux\f[R], \f[B]aarch64\-linux\f[R],
\f[B]riscv64\-linux\f[R], \f[B]powerpc64le\-linux\f[R],
\f[B]s390x\-linux\f[R], \f[B]x86_64\-macos\f[R],
\f[B]aarch64\-macos\f[R], \f[B]x86_64\-freebsd\f[R],
\f[B]aarch64\-freebsd\f[R], \f[B]x86_64\-netbsd\f[R],
\f[B]aarch64\-netbsd\f[R], \f[B]x86_64\-openbsd\f[R], and
\f[B]aarch64\-openbsd\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-install\-target\f[R]
When using \f[B]\-\-target\f[R], download and install the target
platform\[cq]s libraries without asking for confirmation.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]TOMO_PATH\f[R]
The installation prefix Tomo compiles and links against (its
\f[B]lib/\f[R], \f[B]include/\f[R], \f[B]libexec/\f[R], and
\f[B]man/\f[R] trees) and installs into.
By default this is determined at runtime from the location of the
running \f[B]tomo\f[R] binary \[em] the grandparent of the resolved
executable path \[em] so an installation is relocatable.
Set \f[B]TOMO_PATH\f[R] to override it, for example to point at a tree
in an unusual location or one whose \f[B]tomo\f[R] binary has been
copied away from its sibling directories.
.TP
\f[B]TOMO_DIST_URL\f[R]
Base URL for downloading distribution archives (used by
\f[B]\-\-target\f[R] and \f[B]\-\-install\-target\f[R] to fetch a target
platform\[cq]s libraries).
Defaults to \f[I]https://tomo.bruce\-hill.com/dist\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]EDITOR\f[R]
The editor bare \f[B]tomo\f[R] opens the scratch file in.
Defaults to \f[B]vim\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]NO_COLOR\f[R], \f[B]COLOR\f[R]
Set \f[B]NO_COLOR\f[R] to any non\-empty value to disable colored
output.
Set \f[B]COLOR\f[R] to \f[B]1\f[R] to force it on; otherwise color is
used only when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
\f[B]TOMO_STACKTRACE\f[R], \f[B]TOMO_PLAIN_ERRORS\f[R], \f[B]TOMO_CORE_DUMP\f[R]
Diagnostics for compiler and runtime errors: \f[B]TOMO_STACKTRACE\f[R]
prints a stack trace on errors, \f[B]TOMO_PLAIN_ERRORS\f[R] disables the
fancy source\-quoting error formatting, and setting
\f[B]TOMO_CORE_DUMP\f[R] to a truthy value makes a fatal error abort
(dumping core) instead of exiting cleanly.
.TP
\f[B]TOMO_TEST_FILTER\f[R], \f[B]TOMO_TEST_TIMEOUT\f[R], \f[B]TOMO_TEST_VERBOSE\f[R]
Control \f[B]tomo test\f[R] runs: only run tests whose label contains
\f[B]TOMO_TEST_FILTER\f[R], cap each test at \f[B]TOMO_TEST_TIMEOUT\f[R]
seconds, and print verbose output when \f[B]TOMO_TEST_VERBOSE\f[R] is
set.
.TP
\f[B]ZIG_GLOBAL_CACHE_DIR\f[R]
Where the bundled Zig toolchain keeps its global compile cache.
If unset, Tomo points it at its own cache directory (under
\f[B]$XDG_CACHE_HOME/tomo\f[R]) rather than Zig\[cq]s default, so it
never mingles with a separately\-run Zig\[cq]s cache; an explicit value
is respected.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]prefix\f[R]\f[B]/bin/tomo\[at]\f[R]\f[I]version\f[R]
The compiler binary, with a \f[B]bin/tomo\f[R] symlink to the newest
coresident version.
Programs installed with \f[B]tomo build \-\-install\f[R] also land in
\f[I]prefix\f[R]\f[B]/bin/\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]prefix\f[R]\f[B]/lib/tomo\[at]\f[R]\f[I]version\f[R]\f[B]/\f[R], \f[I]prefix\f[R]\f[B]/include/tomo\[at]\f[R]\f[I]version\f[R]\f[B]/\f[R]
The Tomo standard library archive and headers this version links
against.
.TP
\f[I]prefix\f[R]\f[B]/man/man1/\f[R]
Man pages, including those installed for programs built with \f[B]tomo
build \-\-install\f[R].
